=== Sound propagation radius ===
[[File:Noteblock radius.png|thumb|A representation of Minecraft's noteblock sound propagation. In red, the sound that reaches the player; in yellow the sound that activates warden tendril; and in blue the sound that activates sculk sensors.]]
The sound of note blocks reaches different radii depending on the target:

* [[Player]]s can hear a note block being activated up to a radius of 48 blocks.
* [[Warden]]s and [[allay]]s can hear a note block being activated up to a radius of 16 blocks.
* [[Sculk sensor]]s can detect a note block being activated up to a radius of 8 blocks.

=== Redstone === 
{{main|Tutorial:Redstone music}}

==== Activating note blocks ====
[[File:NoteBlockRedstone.png|thumb|A pair of note blocks wired to a [[clock circuit]] to play alternately]]

Note blocks can be activated in a variety of different ways, following normal redstone principles. Note blocks [[conductive block|conduct]] redstone power, which means they activate blocks next to them when directly powered, including other note blocks. 

{{IN|je}}, after activating a note block or changing its pitch (via {{control|use}}), the change is treated as a block state change. This immediately plays the sound and generates a block update detectable by both [[observer]]s and [[block update detector]]s (BUDs). An observer facing a note block will trigger whenever the note block is {{Control|use|text=used}}, powered by redstone, or its instrument/pitch changes. Because note blocks conduct power, they can be integrated into complex observer-based circuits as conditional triggers or signal routers.

{{IN|be}}, due to a known bug,<ref>{{bug|MCPE-53919}}</ref> activating a note block or changing its pitch (via {{control|use}}) does not trigger a block update, meaning neither [[observer]]s nor [[BUD]]s can detect the change. This occurs because pitch and instrument data are stored as [[block entity]] data, which updates independently of the main world loop. In circuits requiring a redstone-triggered or manually activated block updates (e.g., flying machines), [[bells]] are often used as substitutes in most situations.

==== Sculk detection ====
Note blocks emit vibrations detectable by [[sculk sensor]]s. A [[calibrated sculk sensor]] tuned to signal strength 10 detects a note playing.

{{IN|be}}, sculk sensors provide the only reliable method of detecting both note block activations and pitch changes for redstone, since observers and BUDs cannot register these events.

=== Allay interactions ===
If an [[allay]] hears a note block play within 16 blocks of its location, it pathfinds to it. It then spends 30 seconds near that note block, seeking items and dropping them near the note block instead of its player. After 30 seconds, the allay returns to targeting its player unless the note block is played again. A [[Particles#vibration|vibration particle]] emanates from the note block and reaches the allay to indicate the allay has locked onto it. Placing [[wool]] between the note block and the allay prevents this [[Sculk Sensor#Vibration detection|interaction]]. Allays ignore note blocks which do not make sounds, with the exception of note blocks with [[Player Head|player heads]] on top.

=== Fuel ===
Note blocks can be used as [[fuel]] in [[furnace]]s, smelting 1.5 items per block.

